(module crux-probe-polarity-inverted)
;; THE MUTANT HALF DROPPED. Stage H's bar is two-sided: the probe body must
;; verify body-faithfully AND its mutant must fail to verify. This body keeps
;; the probe half and drops the mutant conjunct, so a probe whose mutant
;; SURVIVES is scored as establishing feasibility.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS THE FAILURE THAT MATTERS. The probe half alone shows only that
;; the contract shape is satisfiable. It says nothing about whether the
;; contract EXCLUDES anything, and a contract that excludes nothing verifies
;; every implementation including the historically-attested bug it was written
;; against. Stage H exists to catch exactly that before the swarm spends a wave
;; authoring bodies against a decorative shape, so scoring only the good half
;; turns the gate into a formality that always passes.
;;
;; This is the same defect class as a test suite with no failing case: the
;; measurement is real, and it measures nothing about the property claimed.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [H9-PROBE-SAFE] + [H9-PROBE-FAITHFUL] + [H9-NO-EXTRA-BAR]  -> SAFE
;;   [H9-MUTANT-REFUTED] alone                                  -> REFUTED
;;
;; [H9-NO-EXTRA-BAR] survives and that is the informative half. This mutant is
;; strictly WEAKER than the honest decision, so the converse implication still
;; holds: every genuinely established probe does pass. An anti-hardcoding post
;; cannot catch a scorer that accepts too much, only one that accepts too
;; little. The forward post is what covers this direction.
(def probe-established? [probe-safe: bool
                         probe-body-faithful: bool
                         mutant-safe: bool] -> bool

  (post (=> result probe-safe))
  (post (=> result probe-body-faithful))
  (post (=> result (not mutant-safe)))

  (post (=> (and probe-safe
                 (and probe-body-faithful (not mutant-safe))) result))

  (and probe-safe probe-body-faithful))
